 Compile and install the pywrapper setuid C binary, and set up program entries
 that execute Python scripts as a common or effective user via the setuid mechanism.
 Must be run inside the target Python virtual environment.

 Usage: pywrapper-install [-n|--username USERNAME] [-e|--envhome ENVHOME]
                          ( -c|--compile
                          | -p|--pgstart
                          | -l|--link PROGRAM|all [-s|--simple]
                          | -m|--cmlink PROGRAM [-s|--simple] [-t|--target SCRIPT]
                                                 [-d|--destdir DIR]
                          | -u|--update )

 Run pywrapper-install with no arguments to display this user guide.  Exactly
 one of -c/--compile, -p/--pgstart, -l/--link, -m/--cmlink, or -u/--update
 must be given to perform an action.

      - Option -n or --username USERNAME
          The user name to own the setuid binary.  For Mode 1 (CommonUser program),
          this is the common user (e.g. gdexdata).  For Mode 2 (pgstart), this is
          the specialist user (e.g. zji).  Defaults to 'gdexdata' unless -p/--pgstart
          is given, in which case it defaults to the current login user.

      - Option -e or --envhome ENVHOME
          Path to the venv root directory (the parent of bin/).  Defaults to the
          parent of the currently active Python executable's bin/ directory.
          Only needed when installing into an environment other than the one
          currently active.

      - Option -c or --compile
          Compile pywrapper.c (bundled with this package) and install it as
          bin/pywrapper, owned by USER with chmod 4750 (setuid).  Must be done
          once per environment before using -p/--pgstart or -l/--link.  Cannot
          be combined with -p/--pgstart or -l/--link.

      - Option -p or --pgstart
          Mode 2: copy the compiled pywrapper binary to pgstart_USER (owned by USER,
          chmod 4750).  Allows USER (any login user in the same group as the
          common user PGLOG['COMMONUSER']) to run arbitrary commands as themselves
          via the setuid wrapper.  Two paths for setting up pgstart_USER:
            (a) if PGLOG['ADMINUSER'] (default zji) can 'sudo -u USER', the admin
                runs 'pywrapper-install -p -n USER' on behalf of USER; or
            (b) USER runs the same command themselves (no sudo required).
          Cannot be combined with -c/--compile or -l/--link.

      - Option -l or --link PROGRAM|all
          Mode 1: create a symlink PROGRAM -> pywrapper in the bin/ directory so
          that running PROGRAM invokes the setuid wrapper, which then execs
          setuid_PROGRAM as CommonUser.  setuid_PROGRAM keeps its default
          ownership and permissions.  Cannot be combined with -c/--compile or
          -p/--pgstart.
          Use 'all' instead of a program name to scan bin/ for every setuid_*
          entry and add any missing PROGRAM -> pywrapper symlinks in one pass.

      - Option -m or --cmlink PROGRAM
          Mode 3: compile cmwrapper.c (bundled with this package) into a dedicated
          binary named PROGRAM, owned by USER with chmod 4755 (setuid), so that
          users who are NOT in the common user's group can run PROGRAM as
          CommonUser.  Unlike pywrapper, the script to exec is baked into the
          binary at compile time and the environment is replaced with a fixed
          whitelist, so the binary cannot be symlinked under another name to reach
          a different program, and PYTHONPATH, LD_PRELOAD or the PGLOG path
          variables cannot be used to run code as CommonUser.  One binary is
          compiled per program.  Cannot be combined with the other actions.

          Only wrap a program that does its own authorization and confines where
          it writes, such as gdexdrop; never wrap a general purpose program such
          as gdexcp, which would let any user on the machine read or overwrite
          any file of the common user.

          Add -s/--simple to compile the binary 755 with no setuid, which is how
          a program of this environment is published to users who do not have the
          environment at all, such as gdexls.  There is no privilege change, so
          the caller's environment is kept except for PYTHONPATH, PYTHONHOME and
          PYTHONSTARTUP, which would send the program to another environment's
          modules.  The default -t/--target is then bin/PROGRAM, and no sudo
          access to USER is needed.

      - Option -t or --target SCRIPT (use with -m/--cmlink)
          Absolute path of the python script the cmwrapper binary execs.
          Defaults to bin/setuid_PROGRAM of the environment, or to bin/PROGRAM
          with -s/--simple.

      - Option -d or --destdir DIR (use with -m/--cmlink)
          Directory to install the cmwrapper binary into, typically a common area
          on everyone's PATH, such as /glade/u/home/gdexdata/bin.  Defaults to the
          bin/ directory of the environment.

      - Option -u or --update
          Update an existing installation.  Discovers all pgstart_* and pywrapper
          binaries already present in bin/, saves them to a temporary update_tmp/
          subdirectory, recompiles pywrapper using update_tmp/pgstart_COMMONUSER, and
          reinstalls every pgstart_* binary from update_tmp.  Use -n/--username to
          specify the gdex common user (default: gdexdata).

      - Option -s or --simple (use with -l/--link or -m/--cmlink)
          Simple install: skip the setuid mechanism entirely, so that the program
          runs as the current user with no privilege change.  -n/--username is not
          required with this option.  With -l/--link, a symlink PROGRAM ->
          setuid_PROGRAM is created for users who do not need or cannot set up the
          setuid wrapper; with -m/--cmlink, a 755 cmwrapper binary is compiled for
          users who do not have this environment at all.

 Convention for wrapped programs:
   Any Python package whose program is to be run via pywrapper must:

   1. Declare rda_python_setuid as a dependency in pyproject.toml:

         [project]
         dependencies = [
           "rda_python_setuid",
           ...
         ]

      This ensures pywrapper-install is available in the environment after
      pip install.

   2. Register its connector entry point with a setuid_ prefix in pyproject.toml:

         [project.scripts]
         "setuid_dsarch" = "rda_python_dsarch.dsarch:main"

      pip install then places setuid_dsarch in the bin/ directory automatically.
      Running pywrapper-install --link creates the symlink dsarch -> pywrapper;
      pywrapper (running as CommonUser via its setuid bit) execs setuid_dsarch.

 Environment Setup:

   Option A - Python venv (recommended for DECS machines):

      1. Create and activate the virtual environment:
            python3 -m venv $ENVHOME
            source $ENVHOME/bin/activate

      2. Install the required packages:
            pip install rda_python_setuid rda_python_dsarch ...

      3. Deactivate when done:
            deactivate

      $ENVHOME is typically /glade/u/home/gdexdata/gdexmsenv on DECS machines.

   Option B - Conda (recommended for DAV/Casper):

      1. Create and activate the conda environment:
            conda create -n pg-gdex python=3.10
            conda activate pg-gdex

      2. Install the required packages:
            pip install rda_python_setuid rda_python_dsarch ...

      The conda environment is typically located at:
            /glade/work/gdexdata/conda-envs/pg-gdex

      Use 'conda activate pg-gdex' to activate it before running pywrapper-install.

 Setup sequence for a new environment:

   Option A - Full setuid setup (requires sudo access to CommonUser):

   1. Install the target package and its dependencies:
         pip install rda_python_dsarch

   2. Compile pywrapper (once per environment):
         pywrapper-install -c

   3. Wire up each program as a setuid entry:
         pywrapper-install -l dsarch        # one program
         pywrapper-install -l all           # or all setuid_* entries at once

   4. Optionally, install a pgstart_<loginname> binary so <loginname> (any
      user in the same group as PGLOG['COMMONUSER']) can run commands as
      themselves.  Either PGLOG['ADMINUSER'] (default zji, if it has
      'sudo -u <loginname>'), or <loginname> directly, runs:
         pywrapper-install -p -n <loginname>

   Option B - Simple install (no sudo required, runs as current user):

   1. Install the target package:
         pip install rda_python_dsarch

   2. Create a direct symlink to the connector script:
         pywrapper-install -l dsarch -s     # one program
         pywrapper-install -l all -s        # or all setuid_* entries at once

 Examples:

   1. Compile and install pywrapper for common user gdexdata (default):
         pywrapper-install -c

   2. Same, but targeting a specific environment:
         pywrapper-install -c -e /glade/u/home/gdexdata/gdexmsenv

   3. Wire up dsarch to run as gdexdata via pywrapper:
         pywrapper-install -l dsarch

   4. Wire up all setuid_* entries at once:
         pywrapper-install -l all

   5. Install pgstart_zji so login user zji can run commands as themselves
      via pgstart (run by ADMINUSER with 'sudo -u zji' available, or by zji):
         pywrapper-install -p -n zji

   6. Update an existing installation (recompile and reinstall all setuid binaries):
         pywrapper-install -u

   7. Simple install of dsarch for a user who does not need setuid:
         pywrapper-install -l dsarch -s

   8. Simple install of all setuid_* entries at once:
         pywrapper-install -l all -s

   9. Let users outside the gdexdata group run gdexdrop as gdexdata, installing
      the 4755 binary into a common area on everyone's PATH:
         pywrapper-install -m gdexdrop -d /glade/u/home/gdexdata/bin

  10. Publish gdexls of this environment to users who have no environment of
      their own, with no setuid and no privilege change:
         pywrapper-install -m gdexls -s -d /glade/u/apps/contrib
